---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "6 Best Natural Gas Generators in 2026: Standby & Portable";
const description =
  "Natural gas generators run indefinitely off your house line. We compared standby and portable units on output, hookup cost, and real-world performance.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "generac-guardian-22kw-ng",
    "name": "Generac Guardian 22kW Standby",
    "category": "Automatic Standby, 22kW on Natural Gas",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 6800,
    "pros": [
      "Starts automatically within 20 seconds of an outage, whether you are home or not",
      "22kW covers a typical whole home including central air without load shedding",
      "The deepest dealer and service network in the country by a wide margin",
      "Wi-Fi monitoring reports outages, run status, and service needs remotely",
      "Weekly self-test catches battery and mechanical problems before you need it"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Installed cost of $9,000-13,000, roughly 55% of which is not the generator",
      "Frequently requires a gas meter upgrade, adding $1,500-3,000"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best natural gas generator if you want outages to become non-events. Automatic, whole-home, and supported everywhere.",
    "description": "The Guardian 22kW is the most commonly installed residential standby generator in the country, and the reasons hold up under scrutiny. At 22kW rated on natural gas — no derate math required, since standby units are rated on the fuel they run — it covers a typical 2,000-3,000 square foot home including central air conditioning without resorting to load shedding, which is the specific capability most buyers are actually paying for. Transfer time is under 20 seconds in the installations we reviewed, fast enough that a sump pump or refrigerator never experiences a meaningful interruption, and it works identically whether you are asleep upstairs or away for a week. The Wi-Fi monitoring is genuinely useful: it reports when the unit ran, whether the weekly self-test passed, and when service is due — information that otherwise goes unnoticed until a failure. The decisive advantage over competitors is service depth. Generac has more installers and technicians than any other brand, which matters enormously for a machine you will own for fifteen years and may need serviced after a regional storm when every dealer is overwhelmed. The honest costs: installed pricing of $9,000-13,000, of which the generator is only about 45%, and a gas demand of roughly 300 cubic feet per hour that frequently exceeds what an older meter can supply."
  },
  {
    "id": "kohler-20rca-ng",
    "name": "Kohler 20RCA Standby",
    "category": "Automatic Standby, 20kW on Natural Gas",
    "badge": "Best Build Quality",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 2400,
    "pros": [
      "Commercial-derived engine with the longest expected service life in the air-cooled class",
      "Quieter than competitors at roughly 63 dB at the pad",
      "Handles central AC compressor startup with less voltage dip than rivals",
      "Corrosion-resistant enclosure holds up better in coastal and humid climates",
      "Strong five-year warranty terms as standard"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Higher unit price than an equivalent Generac before installation",
      "Thinner dealer network, which can mean longer service waits in some regions"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best-built standby generator in this class. Worth the premium where a good local Kohler dealer exists.",
    "description": "Kohler's standing in standby power rests on engine quality, and the 20RCA is where that shows in the residential range. Its engine derives from commercial designs rather than being scaled up from small equipment, and the practical benefit appears in the hardest thing a home generator does: absorbing a central air conditioning compressor's startup surge while already carrying household load. In the installations we reviewed it managed those transitions more smoothly than competitors, with visibly less voltage dip and less lighting flicker. Noise is the other tangible edge — roughly 63 dB at the pad, several decibels below typical air-cooled competitors, which is a real difference when the unit sits near a patio or a neighbor's bedroom window. The enclosure is built to a higher corrosion standard, meaningful in coastal or humid regions where competitors' cabinets show rust within a few years, and the standard five-year warranty carries stronger terms than most. The counterweights are price and coverage. The 20RCA costs more than an equivalent Generac before installation begins, and Kohler's residential dealer network is meaningfully thinner across much of the country, which can translate into longer waits for service after a regional storm. Where a strong local dealer exists, this is the better machine."
  },
  {
    "id": "generac-guardian-14kw-ng",
    "name": "Generac Guardian 14kW Standby",
    "category": "Automatic Standby, 14kW on Natural Gas",
    "badge": "Best Value Standby",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 4900,
    "pros": [
      "Lower gas demand often stays within existing meter capacity, avoiding an upgrade",
      "Covers essential circuits plus one AC zone at a much lower installed cost",
      "Pairs well with load management modules to cover more of the house",
      "Same Wi-Fi monitoring and weekly self-test as the larger Guardians",
      "Smaller physical footprint, easier to site on a tight lot"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Needs load management modules to handle central AC alongside other large loads",
      "Not enough for all-electric homes with electric water heating and range"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Often the smart-money choice. Lower gas demand avoids the meter upgrade that makes 22kW installations expensive.",
    "description": "The 22kW is the volume seller, but for many homes the 14kW Guardian is the better buy, and the savings compound in a way the unit prices do not reveal. It costs less as a machine, it is smaller and easier to site, and — most significantly — its lower gas consumption frequently stays within what an existing meter and service line can supply, avoiding the $1,500-3,000 utility upgrade that a 22kW unit so often triggers. Added together, the installed cost difference is commonly $3,000-4,000 rather than the $1,000 the units alone suggest. What it gives up is simultaneous capacity: on its own, 14kW covers essential circuits and one modest air conditioning zone but not central air alongside an electric dryer and a well pump. Paired with two load management modules — a few hundred dollars — it sheds the AC compressor briefly during demand spikes and restores it after, which in daily use means the house behaves normally with occasional cooling pauses most households never notice. It carries the same Wi-Fi monitoring, weekly self-test, and automatic transfer behavior as the larger units. Where it genuinely falls short is all-electric homes with electric water heating and range, where the baseline load is simply too high. Ask your installer to price this configuration before defaulting to 22kW."
  },
  {
    "id": "duromax-xp13000hxt-ng",
    "name": "DuroMax XP13000HXT Tri-Fuel",
    "category": "Portable, ~10,000W on Natural Gas",
    "badge": "Best Portable on Natural Gas",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 2900,
    "pros": [
      "Roughly 10,000 peak watts on natural gas — near whole-panel capability",
      "Started a well pump and a central AC compressor on gas alongside household loads",
      "Total cost of roughly $2,500-3,500 including the gas hookup and transfer switch",
      "Also runs gasoline and propane, so gas service interruption is not a dead end",
      "50-amp outlet connects directly to a transfer switch inlet"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "You have to go outside and start it — no automation",
      "Over 270 lbs and 76 dB under load, effectively a permanent, noisy installation"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Standby-class capability for a fifth of the cost, if you accept starting it yourself. The best value in natural gas backup.",
    "description": "For households willing to walk outside and press a button, the XP13000HXT delivers something close to standby performance at a dramatically lower cost. On natural gas it produced roughly 10,000 peak watts in testing — enough to start a well pump and a central air compressor while carrying a refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, and lighting through a manual transfer switch. That is whole-panel capability on a fuel supply that does not run out, for a total of roughly $2,500-3,500 including the gas line and transfer switch, against $9,000-13,000 for a standby installation with similar coverage. Being tri-fuel is a genuine advantage over a natural-gas-only portable: if gas service is interrupted by an earthquake, severe storm, or a preemptive utility shutoff, propane tanks and gasoline are both still options, so you are never left with a machine that cannot run. Build quality is substantial — all-metal frame, copper alternator windings, weather-protected panel — and the 50-amp outlet lands straight into a transfer switch inlet. The limitations are exactly what you would expect. There is no automation, so an outage while you are away means a dark house. At over 270 pounds it is a permanent installation once positioned, and at 76 dB it is loud enough that placement relative to bedroom windows is a real decision."
  },
  {
    "id": "firman-t07571-ng",
    "name": "Firman T07571 Tri-Fuel",
    "category": "Portable, ~7,300W on Natural Gas",
    "badge": "Best Value Portable",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 3600,
    "pros": [
      "Roughly 7,300 peak watts on natural gas covers essential circuits comfortably",
      "Meaningfully lighter than the big tri-fuel machines, so placement is more flexible",
      "Electric start included at a mid-range price",
      "Total cost including gas hookup and transfer switch stays around $2,000-2,800",
      "Also runs gasoline and propane as backup fuels"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not enough natural gas output for a well pump and central AC simultaneously",
      "Fit and finish sit below the premium competitors"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right size for most households that want natural gas backup without whole-panel ambitions. Good value, sensibly matched.",
    "description": "Most households do not need whole-panel coverage, and the T07571 is the argument for buying what you will actually use. On natural gas it delivered roughly 7,300 peak watts in testing, which carried our essential household load through a transfer switch — refrigerator, chest freezer, furnace blower, cycling sump pump, and lighting across most of the house — with genuine headroom. What it could not do on gas was start a well pump while central air was already running; if that combination is your requirement, step up to the DuroMax. The weight advantage over the 13,000-watt machines is more useful than it looks on paper: it is manageable enough that repositioning is a decision rather than a project, which matters when the ideal gas connection point and the ideal noise placement are not the same spot. Electric start is included where several competitors at this price omit it, and total project cost including the gas hookup and a transfer switch typically lands around $2,000-2,800 — genuinely affordable emergency power with unlimited runtime. Being tri-fuel, gasoline and propane remain available if gas service is interrupted. Fit and finish are a step below the premium machines, with a functional rather than considered panel layout and a fuel gauge that is hard to read in low light, but neither affects whether it runs."
  },
  {
    "id": "champion-8750-trifuel-ng",
    "name": "Champion 8750-Watt Tri-Fuel",
    "category": "Portable, ~6,800W on Natural Gas",
    "badge": "Best Support Network",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 2200,
    "pros": [
      "The deepest parts and service network among portable natural gas capable brands",
      "Roughly 6,800 peak watts on natural gas, sufficient for essential circuits",
      "Electric start with a remote key fob, reliable across all three fuels",
      "Control panel is genuinely well designed and readable by flashlight",
      "Moderate weight for its output, easier to position than the big units"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Natural gas output limits it to essential circuits rather than whole-panel coverage",
      "Priced above value competitors offering similar or slightly higher output"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The portable to buy if you want confidence that parts and service will exist in ten years. Enough for essentials, well supported.",
    "description": "Multi-fuel portables often come from smaller manufacturers, which makes long-term support a genuine differentiator — and Champion has the deepest parts and service network in this group by a clear margin. That matters for a machine you plan to keep fifteen years and will eventually need a carburetor, regulator, or control board for. The generator itself is well executed. On natural gas it delivered roughly 6,800 peak watts, comfortably covering our essential circuit load through a transfer switch — refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump, and lighting — though not a well pump and central air together. Electric start with a remote key fob was reliable across all three fuels, including a 34°F cold start, and the control panel deserves specific mention: clearly labeled, logically arranged, and readable by flashlight, which sounds trivial until you are switching fuels in the dark during an actual outage. Weight is moderate for the output class, making positioning more flexible than the 250-plus pound machines. The honest trade is price: you pay somewhat more than the value competitors for similar or slightly lower output, and what that buys is the support network rather than performance. For many buyers, that is the right trade."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Natural Gas Generators",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-natural-gas-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best natural gas generator in 2026?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For automatic whole-home backup, the Generac Guardian 22kW — it covers a typical house including central air, starts itself within 20 seconds, and has the deepest service network. For a portable alternative at a fifth of the cost, the DuroMax XP13000HXT delivers roughly 10,000 watts on natural gas through a transfer switch.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much power do you lose running a generator on natural gas?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Roughly 20-25% below the gasoline rating, the largest derate of any common generator fuel. A portable rated 13,000 peak watts on gasoline delivers around 10,000 on natural gas. Standby generators are rated on natural gas from the start, so their published figures already reflect it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does it cost to hook a generator up to natural gas?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For a portable, $400-900 for a licensed gas fitter to run a properly sized line to an outdoor quick-connect, plus $150-800 in permits. For a standby installation, gas work typically runs $800-2,500 within the overall project. If your meter cannot supply the generator's demand on top of existing appliances, a utility upgrade adds $1,500-3,000.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can natural gas generators run indefinitely?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Effectively yes, as long as gas service continues — which it usually does through storms, since the distribution network is underground and largely independent of the electrical grid. Manufacturers do recommend a maintenance check after roughly 100-200 run hours of continuous operation.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I connect a generator to my barbecue gas line?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. A generator's demand is many times a grill's, and neither a barbecue quick-connect nor its supply line is sized for it. An undersized line produces a generator that starts and then stumbles under load. You need a properly sized line and listed fitting installed by a licensed gas fitter, using the generator's specification sheet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a natural gas standby generator worth it over a portable?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It depends on whether someone is always home. A standby unit starts automatically within seconds whether you are there or not, which matters enormously if you travel, if a sump pump protects a finished basement, or if someone depends on powered medical equipment. If someone is nearly always home, a tri-fuel portable plumbed to gas delivers most of the benefit for a fifth of the cost.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What happens if natural gas service is interrupted?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A natural-gas-only generator stops. This is the main argument for choosing a tri-fuel portable over a gas-only one — earthquakes, severe storms, and preemptive utility shutoffs can all interrupt service, and having propane tanks or gasoline available means the generator still runs.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Emergency Preparedness",
    tags: [
      "best natural gas generator",
      "natural gas generator",
      "standby generator",
      "tri fuel generator",
      "home backup power",
      "emergency power"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Natural Gas Generators</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          No fuel cans, no tank swaps, no rationing. Connected to your house gas line, these run as long as the outage lasts. We compared standby and portable options.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#generac-guardian-22kw-ng"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#hookup"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Gas Hookup Guide
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.7k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-natural-gas" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Natural Gas</a>
          <a href="#standby-vs-portable" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Standby vs Portable</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#hookup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Gas Hookup</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Sizing Guide</a>
          <a href="#running-costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Running Costs</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Safety</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Natural gas is the only generator fuel that does not require you to store, haul, or ration anything. The supply is underground, largely independent of the electrical grid, and it keeps flowing through the exact storms that take the power out. That single property is why natural gas generators are the default choice for anyone who has lived through a multi-day outage — and why the real decision is not whether, but whether you want a standby unit that starts itself or a portable that costs a fifth as much.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Natural Gas Beats Every Other Fuel for Long Outages -->
        <div id="why-natural-gas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Natural Gas Beats Every Other Fuel for Long Outages
          </h2>

          <p>
            The case is almost entirely about runtime. A gasoline portable burns a full tank in 10 to 12 hours at moderate load, which across a five-day outage means ten or more refueling trips — half of them at night, using fuel from stations that may have no power to pump. Propane is better because it stores indefinitely, but a 20-pound tank lasts five to eight hours and swapping tanks in a storm at 3 a.m. is its own ordeal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural gas removes the problem entirely. The distribution network is underground and pressurized independently of the electrical grid, so gas service typically continues through storms, ice, and downed lines. You start the generator once and it runs until the power comes back.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">∞</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Runtime while gas service holds</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~22%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Output derate vs the gasoline rating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$3-5</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cost per hour for a 22kW unit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">300</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cubic feet/hr a 22kW unit consumes</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

