        <!-- SECTION 4: The Natural Gas Hookup: Requirements and Cost -->
        <div id="hookup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Natural Gas Hookup: Requirements and Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            The gas connection is where natural gas generator projects go over budget, and it is entirely predictable if you check the right things first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Meter Capacity Before Anything Else</h3>
          <p>
            A 22kW standby generator consumes roughly 300 cubic feet of gas per hour — comparable to running a furnace and water heater simultaneously. Many homes have a meter and service line sized only for existing appliances, and adding a generator exceeds it. Your utility can tell you the meter's rated capacity and what your current appliances draw. If an upgrade is needed, expect $1,500-3,000 and potentially weeks of scheduling delay. Make this verification a written condition before signing any installation contract.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Line Sizing Over the Actual Run</h3>
          <p>
            Gas pressure drops over distance, and a line that is adequate at 20 feet may not be at 60. The pipe must be sized for the generator's demand at the required pressure — typically 5 to 11 inches of water column for portables, per the manufacturer's spec — over the real run length. An undersized line produces a generator that starts fine and then stumbles under load, a genuinely frustrating problem to diagnose after the pipe is buried.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Typical Costs</h3>
          <p>
            For a portable, a licensed gas fitter running a properly sized line to an outdoor quick-connect typically costs $400-900 depending on distance and how much of the run is exposed. For a standby installation, gas work usually runs $800-2,500 and is bundled into the overall project. Permits and inspection add $150-800 in most jurisdictions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Improvise the Connection</h3>
          <p>
            The flexible connector between the quick-connect and a portable generator must be a listed gas connector rated for the pressure and flow, not a barbecue hose with adapters. A grill's demand is a small fraction of a generator's, and the fittings and hose are not designed for it. Use the hose supplied with the generator or buy one rated for the application.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Get a Permit</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Most jurisdictions require a permit and inspection for a new gas line, including a pressure test. An unpermitted gas line is an insurance problem, a disclosure problem when you sell, and — far more importantly — an unverified gas line. This is not a category where saving a few hundred dollars is worth the risk.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

