          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Starting Watts Are Where the Derate Bites</h3>
          <p>
            Motor loads surge on startup — a one-horsepower well pump running at 1,200 watts can demand 4,000 for a second. On natural gas, with roughly 22% less headroom, that surge is what finds the limit. Add running watts for everything on at once, then confirm the natural gas starting rating covers your largest motor's surge on top.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Load Management Can Save a Size</h3>
          <p>
            On standby installations, smart management modules shed non-critical loads — usually the AC compressor or electric water heater — when demand spikes, then restore them. Two modules cost a few hundred dollars and can let a 14kW unit serve a house that would otherwise need 22kW, saving thousands including a possible meter upgrade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: What It Costs to Run -->
        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What It Costs to Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Natural gas is the cheapest generator fuel per kilowatt-hour in most markets, which is a secondary benefit behind the runtime advantage but a real one during a long outage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel Cost</h3>
          <p>
            A 22kW standby unit consumes roughly 200 cubic feet per hour at half load and about 300 at full, which at typical 2026 residential rates works out to roughly $3-5 per hour, or $70-120 for a full day of continuous outage. A 9,000-watt portable on natural gas runs closer to $1.50-3 per hour. For comparison, the same generator on propane costs roughly double.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maintenance</h3>
          <p>
            Natural gas burns cleaner than gasoline, so engines stay cleaner and there is no carburetor varnish to deal with — one of the quiet advantages of the fuel. Standby units need annual service or every 100-200 run hours: oil and filter, air filter, spark plugs, and a battery check, at $200-450 through a dealer or $60-150 in parts doing it yourself. Portables follow the same intervals scaled to their smaller engines.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Battery Is the Weak Point</h3>
          <p>
            On standby units, a dead starting battery is by a wide margin the most common cause of a no-start during an actual outage — the same failure mode as a car. Budget $80-150 every three to four years, and treat a battery warning on the controller or app as urgent rather than informational.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exercise Runs</h3>
          <p>
            Standby generators self-test weekly for a few minutes, costing a few dollars a month in gas. Portables need you to do it manually — 20 to 30 minutes under load every month or two. With a plumbed gas connection this is easy, and it is the single habit that prevents most no-start failures.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Safety -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Clean-Burning Is Not the Same as Safe Indoors</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Natural gas produces less carbon monoxide than gasoline but still produces it in lethal quantities. Every portable generator, on any fuel, must run outdoors at least 20 feet from windows, doors, and vents, with exhaust directed away from the house. Standby units have their own clearance requirements set by code and the manufacturer. Install battery-powered CO detectors inside the home regardless.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Connection Discipline</h3>
          <p>
            Soap-test the connection each time you attach a portable to the quick-connect, know where the shutoff for that line is, and close it when the generator is disconnected. Inspect the flexible connector annually for cracking, abrasion, or corrosion at the fittings and replace it on the manufacturer's schedule.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Backfeed</h3>
          <p>
            Connecting a portable generator to house wiring through a double-male cord energizes the utility line outside your home and can kill the crew repairing the outage, in addition to bypassing every breaker in your panel. Use a manual transfer switch or an approved interlock kit — $500-1,500 installed — or run extension cords directly to appliances. There is no acceptable shortcut here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weather Protection</h3>
          <p>
            A portable running through a multi-day storm needs to stay dry and elevated. Use a purpose-made generator canopy that maintains airflow and exhaust clearance, never a tarp draped over it, which traps exhaust heat and creates a fire risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best natural gas generator in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For automatic whole-home backup, the Generac Guardian 22kW — it covers a typical house including central air, starts itself within 20 seconds, and has the deepest service network. For a portable alternative at a fifth of the cost, the DuroMax XP13000HXT delivers roughly 10,000 watts on natural gas through a transfer switch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much power do you lose running a generator on natural gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly 20-25% below the gasoline rating, the largest derate of any common generator fuel. A portable rated 13,000 peak watts on gasoline delivers around 10,000 on natural gas. Standby generators are rated on natural gas from the start, so their published figures already reflect it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to hook a generator up to natural gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a portable, $400-900 for a licensed gas fitter to run a properly sized line to an outdoor quick-connect, plus $150-800 in permits. For a standby installation, gas work typically runs $800-2,500 within the overall project. If your meter cannot supply the generator's demand on top of existing appliances, a utility upgrade adds $1,500-3,000.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can natural gas generators run indefinitely?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Effectively yes, as long as gas service continues — which it usually does through storms, since the distribution network is underground and largely independent of the electrical grid. Manufacturers do recommend a maintenance check after roughly 100-200 run hours of continuous operation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I connect a generator to my barbecue gas line?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A generator's demand is many times a grill's, and neither a barbecue quick-connect nor its supply line is sized for it. An undersized line produces a generator that starts and then stumbles under load. You need a properly sized line and listed fitting installed by a licensed gas fitter, using the generator's specification sheet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a natural gas standby generator worth it over a portable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on whether someone is always home. A standby unit starts automatically within seconds whether you are there or not, which matters enormously if you travel, if a sump pump protects a finished basement, or if someone depends on powered medical equipment. If someone is nearly always home, a tri-fuel portable plumbed to gas delivers most of the benefit for a fifth of the cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if natural gas service is interrupted?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A natural-gas-only generator stops. This is the main argument for choosing a tri-fuel portable over a gas-only one — earthquakes, severe storms, and preemptive utility shutoffs can all interrupt service, and having propane tanks or gasoline available means the generator still runs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes With Natural Gas Generators
          </h2>

          <p>
            The costly errors in this category almost all involve the gas side, and all of them are avoidable with one phone call to the utility.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Checking Meter Capacity First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 22kW generator consumes roughly 300 cubic feet per hour, comparable to a furnace and water heater together. Many homes have a meter sized only for existing appliances. When the utility has to upsize, it adds $1,500-3,000 and weeks of delay. Make this a written condition before signing anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing a Portable Against Its Gasoline Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Natural gas costs you 20-25% of rated output, and it lands on the surge capacity that starts a well pump or AC compressor. Size against the natural gas figure, since that is the fuel you will run during any outage worth having a generator for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Connecting Through a Barbecue Quick-Connect</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A grill hose and its supply line cannot deliver a generator's flow, and the result is a machine that starts normally and then stumbles under load — a genuinely maddening problem to diagnose after the pipe is buried. Have a licensed fitter size the line from the generator's spec sheet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Gas-Only Instead of Tri-Fuel for a Portable</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Natural gas is more reliable than the electrical grid but not immune — earthquakes, major storms, and preemptive shutoffs all happen. A tri-fuel portable costs little more and keeps propane and gasoline available as backups, so a gas interruption does not leave you without power.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Standby Battery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dead starting battery is by far the most common reason a standby generator fails to run during an outage. It is a $100 part on an $11,000 system. Replace it every three to four years and treat any battery warning on the controller or app as urgent.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
